      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published by the BMJ Publishing Group Limited. For permission to use (where not already granted under a licence) please go to http://www.bmj.com/company/products-services/rights-and-licensing/",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To investigate the association between factors influencing prostate-specific antigen (PSA) testing prevalence including prostate cancer risk factors (age, ethnicity, obesity) and non-risk factors (social deprivation and comorbidity)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "A cross-sectional database of 136 inner London general practices from 1 August 2009 to 31 July 2014."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Men aged ≥40\u2005years without prostate cancer were included (n=150\u2005481)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PRIMARY OUTCOME",
            "content": "Logistic regression analyses were used to estimate the association between PSA testing and age, ethnicity, social deprivation, body mass index (BMI) and comorbidity while adjusting for age, benign prostatic hypertrophy, prostatitis and tamsulosin or finasteride use."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "PSA testing prevalence was 8.2% (2013-2014), and the mean age was 54\u2005years (SD 11). PSA testing was positively associated with age (OR 70-74\u2005years compared to 40-44\u2005years: 7.34 (95% CI 6.82 to 7.90)), ethnicity (black) (OR compared to white: 1.78 (95% CI 1.71 to 1.85)), increasing BMI and cardiovascular comorbidity. Testing was negatively associated with Chinese ethnicity and with increasing social deprivation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "PSA testing among black patients was higher compared to that among white patients, which differs from lower testing rates seen in previous studies. PSA testing was positively associated with prostate cancer risk factors and non-risk factors. Association with non-risk factors may increase the risk of unnecessary invasive diagnostic procedures."
          }
        ]
      },
